## Cycle summary

**Built `cert_incompressibility.py`** — the incompressibility probe the NEXT ACTION called for, answering the **information-theoretic half of open item 3's lower bound**: is the Õ(√x) certificate's size an information floor, or just construction shape (as S510 left open)?

**Key conceptual move.** I separated cert **SIZE** (Θ(√x) just from having K=π(√x) layers) from cert **INFORMATION** (can the K transcripts be jointly compressed?). The chain pins, per layer, the Legendre partial-sieve survivor count `φ(x,a)`; I measured the joint hard bits of `{φ(x,a)}` beyond a polylog smooth predictor, at **integer** precision (the lesson of CLOSED row 737).

**Result — the √x is information-forced for any sieve-reconstructing verifier.** The authoritative, smooth-model-free measure is the integer-reconstruction SVD rank of the residual matrix. Window-sensitivity is decisive: at x=2¹⁹, rank/K climbs **0.09 → 0.97** as the x-sample window grows — the matrix is **full rank** once W≫K, so the K checkpoints are integer-*independent* and the joint info is **Θ(√x)·polylog** (α_rank=0.459 ≈ α_K=0.415 ≈ √x). Per-x gzip/AR exponents (0.39/0.56) corroborate. The single-value control reproduces S36 (|π(x)−Li(x)| is O(log x) bits, slope 0.089) — confirming the barrier is **joint across layers, not per-value**.

**Honest self-correction.** My first run used a fixed narrow window (W=4096) and gave rank ~ x^0.35 (a *sub*-√x reading). The window-sensitivity check refuted this — `rank ≤ min(W,K)` under-samples at large x. Recorded in the results file.

**Precise scope.** This binds the *sieve-reconstruction class* only; a fundamentally different witness (cf. factoring's tiny witness for a hard answer) is not ruled out by information — so a *universal* sub-√x impossibility still routes through **#P-hardness**, which I set as the next action.

